fix(web): show the gene model the server has, not a fixed pair of options (#114) (#120)

* fix(web): say which gene model answered, and stop any client replacing it (#113)

`POST /api/upload-gff3` and `POST /api/load-genome` end in

    self.transcript_provider = IndexedTranscriptProvider::new(transcripts);

so the replacement is process-wide and permanent. On the deployment
`docs/API.md` describes - a lab server several colleagues share, behind a
firewall - one client posting a GFF3 to try a model out changes what
every other client is answered against. A BRCA1 coding variant comes
back `intergenic_variant`, at HTTP 200, with a well-formed body. Both
clients are authorised and neither is doing anything wrong, which is why
the firewall the docs recommend does not help.

It was also undetectable. `/api/status` reported the swap, but only to a
client that polled it between every request; an `/api/annotate` response
carried no indication of what it was computed against.

Two changes, the cheapest first:

**Say what answered.** Every `/api/annotate` response now carries
`gene_model: {gff3_source, transcripts, generation}`, read under the same
read lock that produced the results - so it describes the model that
answered, not whichever one a concurrent upload left behind. `generation`
is the field to compare: it counts replacements, whereas `gff3_source`
reads `user-upload` for every upload and a transcript count can coincide.
Verified: switching from `--gff3 tests/test.gff3` to a data-directory
genome gives the same answer from 48 transcripts both times, and only the
generation tells them apart.

**Make replacing opt-in.** `--allow-model-replacement`, default off,
gates both endpoints with 403 and a message naming the flag. A
single-user desktop run turns it on, because the browser interface's own
genome switching needs it; a shared instance leaves it off and its gene
model becomes a property of how the server was started. `/api/status`
advertises the setting so a UI can offer those controls only when they
work, rather than finding out with a 403 a user cannot interpret.

The authorization check runs before the data-directory check, so no later
refactor of the path logic can let a mutation through.

Per-request gene models - `annotate_vcf_text` taking the model rather
than reading one from `&self` - remain the real fix, and remain worth
scoping only if this API acquires a second real consumer.

Also, from the same review: `resolve_genome_paths` now returns the
per-genome SA directory instead of `load_genome` re-joining it from the
request name. That was safe by ordering rather than by construction - the
traversal check lives in the resolver, and nothing stopped a second join
from being written before it. The traversal test now covers it.

(cherry picked from commit 1182b1e3eedeebf4f9b86a72dc729698e02e4c31)
(cherry picked from commit 9291e64dbf1e29774d01b50471794921df7ed097)

* fix(web): show the gene model the server has, not a fixed pair of options (#114)

The genome selector's two options are written in the HTML, and the report
was that they are all you get. They are the fallbacks: `checkBackend()`
already inserts one option per genome under `--data-dir`, so a server
started with one does offer them. Two things made the list look fixed
anyway, and one of them was worse than cosmetic.

**The model the server already has was not in the list.** Started with
`--gff3 Homo_sapiens.GRCh38.115.gff3`, the selector read "Built-in demo
(OR4F5 + lncRNA, chr1 only)" while the count beside it read 509,650
transcripts - the label and the number describing different models. Worse,
picking that entry meant it: `onGffSourceChange` posted the two-gene demo
over the operator's gene model, for every client of the server, which is
#113's failure with the UI's own default option pulling the trigger.
The active model is now the first entry and the selected one, labelled
from `/api/status`, and selecting it does nothing - the one entry in the
list that is not a replacement.

**A short list did not say why it was short.** No `--data-dir` means no
genomes to switch to, and a server that will not replace its model offers
nothing but the model it has. Both are actionable and neither was stated,
so the remainder read as hardcoded. One line under the selector now names
the flag in each case.

Two consequences of #113 landing in the same area:

- Every option except the active model replaces the gene model, so they
  are disabled when `/api/status` reports `allow_model_replacement:
  false`. Disabled rather than hidden, so the reason stays visible.
- A server that has no gene model and will not accept one answers
  `intergenic_variant` for everything, at HTTP 200.
  `uploadBuiltinToServer` now reports whether the server actually took
  the model, and `annotate()` annotates in the browser when it did not,
  instead of asking a question the server cannot answer.

Verified in headless Chrome against a real server in each of the four
configurations (model or none, replacement allowed or not): the selector
contents, which entries are disabled, the note, that selecting the active
model leaves `gene_model_generation` at 0, that switching to a
data-directory genome still loads it, and that the model-less locked
server annotates client-side - `1:69080 C>T` comes back
`missense_variant` there, where the server alone would have said
`intergenic_variant`.

(cherry picked from commit 018d040e402a21bfb625846ceadfffeaa03d131e)